export const SM1={title:'The sports medicine team, the event and professional responsibilities',hook:'When a high school linebacker collapses on a hot August afternoon, the athletic trainer on the sideline may be the difference between life and death. Sports medicine brings together athletic trainers, team physicians, physical therapists, coaches, psychologists and dietitians to keep athletes healthy, care for injuries and return them to play safely. This lesson introduces the team, the HOSA event and the administrative and legal side of the field.',objectives:['Describe the Sports Medicine event, its test plan and its skills.','Explain the roles of the members of the sports medicine team.','Describe athletic trainer education, certification and domains of practice.','Apply legal, ethical and administrative principles, including emergency action plans and records.'],sections:[
['The event at a glance',[
'Sports Medicine has two rounds. **Round One** is a **50-question test in 60 minutes**: **injury/illness prevention and wellness promotion 30%**, **examination, evaluation and assessment 30%**, **immediate and emergency care 16%**, **therapeutic intervention 16%** and **healthcare administration and professional responsibilities 8%**. References are Beam’s *Orthopedic Taping, Wrapping, Bracing, and Padding*, France’s *Introduction to Sports Medicine and Athletic Training* and Prentice’s *The Role of the Athletic Trainer in Sports Medicine*.',
'**Round Two** may include: **Skill I** anatomical landmark identification (7 minutes—21 landmarks, 15 seconds each, placing a small label on a live patient); **Skill II** joint action and maximum range of motion identification (15 minutes—15 poses or photos; **spelling counts**); and **Skill III** taping/wrapping: **A** ankle (inversion) 5 minutes, **B** Achilles tendon (technique two) 6, **C** shoulder spica 5 and **D** wrist/hand (circular wrist/figure eight) 4. Competitors **bring their own tape, underwrap, elastic wrap, thin foam heel and lace pads with skin lubricant and a shoulder pad**. The 70% recognition threshold applies to the taping skills (excluding the test and Skills I and II); ties go to the higher test score.',
{t:'table',caption:'Written test plan',head:['Domain','Weight'],rows:[['Injury/illness prevention and wellness promotion','30%'],['Examination, evaluation and assessment','30%'],['Immediate and emergency care','16%'],['Therapeutic intervention','16%'],['Healthcare administration and professional responsibilities','8%']]}]],
['The sports medicine team',[
'The **athlete** is at the center. The **athletic trainer (AT)** prevents, recognizes, evaluates and treats injuries and illnesses, provides emergency care and directs rehabilitation, working under the direction of a physician. The **team physician** (often a primary care sports medicine physician or orthopedic surgeon) makes final medical decisions, including **clearance** to participate. **Physical therapists** guide rehabilitation, especially after surgery; **strength and conditioning coaches** design training programs; **sports psychologists** help athletes with the **psychological side of injury** and rehabilitation, motivation and performance; **registered dietitians** advise on nutrition.',
'Others include **coaches** (who often see injuries first), **parents**, **school nurses**, **EMS personnel**, **orthotists**, **massage therapists**, **exercise physiologists**, **chiropractors**, **dentists** and **optometrists**. Communication among team members—and with the athlete—while respecting privacy is essential for safe return to play.',
match('Team member and role',[['Athletic trainer','Prevents, evaluates and treats injuries; emergency care'],['Team physician','Final medical decisions and clearance'],['Sports psychologist','Helps athletes cope with injury and rehabilitation'],['Registered dietitian','Nutrition planning'],['Strength and conditioning coach','Designs training programs'],['Physical therapist','Guides rehabilitation, often after surgery']])]],
['Athletic trainer education and practice',[
'The **National Athletic Trainers’ Association (NATA)** was founded in **1950**. Athletic trainers now complete an accredited **master’s degree** program (the entry-level degree changed from bachelor’s to master’s) accredited by **CAATE**, then pass the **Board of Certification (BOC)** exam to earn the **ATC** credential and meet state licensure or regulation requirements, which exist in nearly every state. ATs maintain certification with continuing education and current emergency cardiac care training.',
'The BOC practice analysis describes five **domains**: **injury and illness prevention and wellness promotion**; **examination, assessment and diagnosis**; **immediate and emergency care**; **therapeutic intervention**; and **healthcare administration and professional responsibility**—the same areas as the HOSA test plan. ATs work in high schools, colleges, professional sports, clinics, the military, performing arts, industry and public safety.',
order('Path to becoming an AT',['High school science and health courses','Accredited master’s program in athletic training','Clinical experiences with athletes','Pass the BOC exam (ATC)','State license or registration','Continuing education and emergency care certification'])]],
['Emergency action plans and pre-participation exams',[
'Every venue needs a written **emergency action plan (EAP)** that lists personnel and roles, **communication** (phones, radios, emergency numbers), **equipment** (AED, splints, spine boards, cold-water immersion tub), **venue directions and access** for EMS (including gate keys and addresses), the nearest appropriate hospital and documentation steps. The plan is **posted**, **rehearsed** at least yearly and reviewed with coaches and staff.',
'A **pre-participation physical examination (PPE)** before the season screens for conditions that could make sport dangerous—cardiac warning signs (fainting during exercise, chest pain, family history of sudden cardiac death), previous injuries, concussions, asthma, sickle cell trait, heat illness history—and documents clearance. **Medical records** include injury reports, treatment logs, rehabilitation notes and return-to-play decisions; they are confidential, accurate and stored securely.',
flip('EAP essentials',[['Personnel','Who does what in an emergency'],['Communication','Phones, radios and emergency numbers'],['Equipment','AED, splints, spine board, cold-water tub'],['Venue access','Address, gates and directions for EMS'],['Rehearsal','Practice the plan at least once a year'],['Documentation','Record what happened and what was done']])]],
['Legal and ethical responsibilities',[
'Athletic trainers must meet the **standard of care**—what a reasonably prudent AT would do. **Negligence** includes **malfeasance** (doing something wrong), **misfeasance** (doing the right thing improperly), **nonfeasance** (failing to act when there is a duty) and acting outside one’s **scope of practice**. Protect yourself and athletes with current credentials, written policies, informed consent, documentation, an EAP and appropriate referral. Many employers carry liability insurance.',
'Privacy laws apply: **HIPAA** governs health information held by healthcare providers, and **FERPA** protects student education records, which can include school health records. Share injury information only as permitted—often with written consent—and never discuss an athlete’s injury with the media without permission. The **NATA Code of Ethics** emphasizes respect, competence, honesty and putting athlete welfare ahead of winning.',
match('Legal terms',[['Standard of care','What a reasonably prudent professional would do'],['Nonfeasance','Failing to act when there is a duty'],['Misfeasance','Doing the right act improperly'],['Malfeasance','Doing an act that should not be done'],['Scope of practice','Legal limits of a professional’s role'],['FERPA','Protects student education records']])]],
['Administration and practice scenario',[
'Administration includes budgeting for supplies (tape, ice, splints, AEDs), maintaining facilities and **sanitation** (clean whirlpools, taping tables and treatment surfaces), ordering and fitting equipment, scheduling coverage, keeping inventory and communicating with administrators, coaches and parents. Good records help justify budgets and improve safety.',
kase('Pressure to return',{a:{text:'A coach wants a star player with concussion symptoms to return in the second half of a championship game. What do you do? (Scenario invented for practice.)',choices:[['Keep the athlete out—no same-day return after a suspected concussion—and refer to the physician','b','Correct. Athlete welfare comes first.',true],['Let the athlete play because it is a championship','b','That violates the standard of care.',false]]},b:{text:'A reporter asks about the player’s injury. What do you say?',choices:[['“I can’t share any health information.”','c','Correct. Privacy rules apply.',true],['Describe the symptoms in detail','c','Health information is confidential.',false]]},c:{text:'You document the evaluation, notify the parents and arrange a physician evaluation.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Round One: prevention and assessment 30% each, emergency care and therapeutic intervention 16% each, administration 8%; Round Two: 21 landmarks, 15 ROM poses (spelling counts) and four taping/wrapping skills with your own supplies.','The AT prevents, evaluates and treats injuries under physician direction; the team physician clears athletes; sports psychologists help athletes cope with injury; ATs earn a master’s degree and the BOC’s ATC.','Every venue needs a rehearsed EAP; pre-participation exams screen risks; meet the standard of care, avoid nonfeasance/misfeasance/malfeasance and protect privacy under HIPAA and FERPA.'],terms:[['ATC','The credential awarded to athletic trainers who pass the BOC exam.'],['Emergency action plan','A written, rehearsed plan for responding to emergencies at a venue.'],['Standard of care','The level of care a reasonably prudent professional would provide.']],mcqs:[q('Which professional can best advise an athlete on the psychological aspects of rehabilitation and coping with injury?','Sports psychologist','Sports physiologist','Sports neurologist','Sports massage therapist','HOSA sample question.'),q('Who makes the final decision on medical clearance?','The team physician','The coach','The parent','The athlete','With input from the AT.'),q('In what year was NATA founded?','1950','1920','1975','2000','Professional association.'),q('Which organization certifies athletic trainers?','Board of Certification (BOC)','NBRC','NCCPA','APTA','ATC credential.'),q('Failing to act when there is a duty is:','Nonfeasance','Malfeasance','Misfeasance','Assumption of risk','Negligence type.'),q('An EAP should be rehearsed at least:','Once a year','Once every 10 years','Never','Only after an emergency','And posted.'),q('Which domain carries 30% of the HOSA test?','Injury/illness prevention and wellness promotion','Healthcare administration','Therapeutic intervention','Immediate care','Also examination 30%.'),q('Which law protects student education records?','FERPA','OSHA','CLIA','EMTALA','Including some school health records.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe the members of a high school sports medicine team and explain how an emergency action plan and good documentation protect athletes and the athletic trainer.',rubric:[['Team members',['athletic trainer','team physician','coach','physical therapist','sports psychologist','dietitian','ems']],['EAP components',['personnel','communication','equipment','aed','venue','directions','hospital','rehearse']],['Documentation',['injury report','treatment','records','confidential','accurate','return to play']],['Legal protection',['standard of care','negligence','scope','consent','hipaa','ferpa']]],model:'A high school sports medicine team centers on the athlete. The athletic trainer prevents, evaluates and treats injuries, provides emergency care and directs rehabilitation under the team physician, who makes final medical and clearance decisions. Coaches often see injuries first and enforce safety rules, physical therapists guide rehabilitation, sports psychologists help athletes cope with injury, registered dietitians advise on nutrition, strength coaches design conditioning and EMS personnel provide transport, with parents and the school nurse also involved. A written emergency action plan lists each person’s role, how to communicate and call EMS, where equipment such as the AED, splints and a cold-water immersion tub are kept, the venue address, gates and directions for ambulances and the nearest hospital, and it is posted and rehearsed at least yearly so the response is fast and coordinated. Good documentation—pre-participation exams, injury reports, treatment logs and return-to-play decisions—creates an accurate, confidential record that supports continuity of care. Together, the EAP and documentation show that the AT met the standard of care, acted within the scope of practice and obtained consent, protecting both the athlete’s health and the AT from negligence claims while respecting HIPAA and FERPA.'},sources:[RULES,NATA,BOC,BLS_AT,NATA_EAP]};

export const SM2={title:'Lower-body anatomy: foot, ankle, lower leg and knee (Skill I)',hook:'In Skill I, a judge calls out a landmark—“anterior talofibular ligament”—and you have 15 seconds to place a small label in exactly the right spot on a live person. Knowing anatomy on paper is not enough; you have to find it on a real body. This lesson covers the foot, ankle, lower leg and knee, where most sports injuries happen.',objectives:['Locate the bones, arches and muscles of the foot.','Identify the bones, ligaments and muscles of the ankle and lower leg.','Identify the bones, ligaments and muscles around the knee.','Relate anatomy to common lower-extremity injuries.'],sections:[
['The foot',[
'The foot has **26 bones**: **7 tarsals** (calcaneus, talus, navicular, cuboid and three cuneiforms), **5 metatarsals** (numbered 1–5 from the big toe) and **14 phalanges** (two in the big toe, three in each other toe). The **base of the fifth metatarsal**, on the outside of the midfoot, is a common fracture site and must not be pressed by tape. The foot’s **arches** absorb shock: the **medial longitudinal arch** (inside), the **lateral longitudinal arch** (outside), the **transverse arch** (across the midfoot) and the **metatarsal arch** (across the metatarsal heads).',
'Muscles on the Skill I list include the **adductor hallucis** (deep in the sole; pulls the big toe toward the second toe), the **flexor and extensor digitorum** (bend and straighten toes 2–5) and the **extensor hallucis longus** (lifts the big toe; its tendon is visible on top of the foot when you raise the big toe). Plantar fascia along the sole supports the medial arch and is irritated in **plantar fasciitis**.',
match('Foot anatomy',[['Calcaneus','Heel bone'],['Talus','Tarsal bone that forms the ankle joint with the tibia and fibula'],['Fifth metatarsal','Outside of the foot; common fracture site'],['Medial longitudinal arch','Inside arch of the foot'],['Extensor hallucis longus','Raises the big toe'],['Phalanges','Toe bones (14 per foot)']])]],
['The ankle and lower leg',[
'The **tibia** (shin bone, medial and weight-bearing) and **fibula** (thin, lateral) form the lower leg; their distal ends are the **medial malleolus** and **lateral malleolus**. The **anterior and posterior tibiofibular ligaments** join the bones above the ankle and are injured in a **high ankle sprain** (syndesmosis). The lateral ankle ligaments are the **anterior talofibular (ATFL)**—the **most commonly sprained ligament**, just in front of the lateral malleolus—the **calcaneofibular (CFL)**, below the malleolus, and the **posterior talofibular (PTFL)**. The strong, fan-shaped **deltoid ligament** on the medial side resists eversion.',
'Muscles: the **gastrocnemius** (two-headed calf muscle crossing the knee) and **soleus** (deeper) join to form the **Achilles tendon**, which inserts on the calcaneus and plantar flexes the ankle. The **tibialis anterior** runs along the front of the shin and **dorsiflexes and inverts**. The **fibularis (peroneus) longus and brevis** run behind the lateral malleolus and **evert** the foot (the brevis inserts on the base of the fifth metatarsal). The **extensor digitorum longus**, **extensor hallucis longus**, **flexor digitorum longus** and **flexor hallucis longus** move the toes and assist ankle motion.',
match('Ankle and lower-leg structures',[['ATFL','Most commonly sprained ankle ligament'],['Calcaneofibular ligament','Below the lateral malleolus'],['Deltoid ligament','Strong medial ankle ligament'],['Tibiofibular ligaments','Injured in a high ankle sprain'],['Fibularis longus and brevis','Evert the foot'],['Tibialis anterior','Dorsiflexes and inverts'],['Achilles tendon','Gastrocnemius and soleus to the calcaneus'],['Soleus','Deep calf muscle under the gastrocnemius']])]],
['The knee',[
'The knee joint connects the **femur** and **tibia**, with the **patella** (kneecap) gliding in a groove on the femur; the **fibula** does not bear knee weight but anchors the **lateral collateral ligament**. Ligaments: the **medial collateral ligament (MCL)** resists **valgus** (knee-in) force; the **lateral collateral ligament (LCL)** resists **varus** (knee-out) force; the **anterior cruciate ligament (ACL)** prevents the tibia from sliding forward; the **posterior cruciate ligament (PCL)** prevents it from sliding backward. The **patellar ligament (tendon)** connects the patella to the tibial tuberosity. **Menisci** cushion the joint.',
'Muscles: the **quadriceps**—**rectus femoris** (also flexes the hip), **vastus lateralis**, **vastus medialis oblique (VMO**, the teardrop above the inside of the kneecap that helps track the patella) and vastus intermedius—extend the knee. The **hamstrings**—**biceps femoris** (lateral), **semitendinosus** and **semimembranosus** (medial)—flex the knee and extend the hip. The **sartorius** (longest muscle, from the front of the hip across to the inside of the knee) and **gracilis** join the semitendinosus at the **pes anserinus**. The **popliteus**, behind the knee, “unlocks” the fully extended knee.',
sort('Medial or lateral?',['Medial side of the knee','Lateral side of the knee'],[['MCL',0],['LCL',1],['Biceps femoris tendon',1],['Semitendinosus tendon',0],['Vastus medialis oblique',0],['Vastus lateralis',1]]),
label('joint','Inside a synovial joint like the knee',[['cartilage','Articular cartilage','Covers the femur and tibia ends.'],['ligament','Ligament','ACL, PCL, MCL and LCL stabilize the knee.'],['capsule','Joint capsule','Encloses the knee joint.'],['synovium','Synovial membrane','Produces joint fluid; swells after injury.'],['bone','Bone','Femur, tibia and patella.']])]],
['Injury links',[
'Anatomy explains injury patterns. **Inversion ankle sprains** (rolling the ankle out) stretch the **ATFL** first, then the CFL. **Eversion sprains** injure the deltoid ligament and can fracture the fibula. The **MCL** is sprained by a **valgus force with rotation on a planted foot**; the **ACL** by **sudden deceleration, cutting, pivoting or landing**; the **PCL** by **falling on a flexed knee** or a dashboard injury. **Achilles tendon ruptures** occur with sudden push-off and are detected by the **Thompson test** (squeezing the calf does not plantar flex the foot).',
'**Medial tibial stress syndrome** (shin splints) causes pain along the inner tibia from overuse; **stress fractures** cause focal bone tenderness; **patellofemoral pain** relates to kneecap tracking and VMO weakness; **Osgood-Schlatter disease** is irritation of the tibial tuberosity in growing adolescents.',
match('Mechanism → injury',[['Rolling the ankle inward (inversion)','ATFL sprain'],['Valgus force with rotation on a planted foot','MCL sprain'],['Sudden deceleration and cutting','ACL tear'],['Falling on a flexed knee','PCL injury'],['Sudden push-off with a pop in the calf','Achilles rupture'],['Painful bump below the kneecap in a growing teen','Osgood-Schlatter disease']])]],
['Practice: find it in 15 seconds',[
'Practice palpation on a partner: find the **lateral malleolus**, slide a finger just in front for the **ATFL**, below for the **CFL**; trace the **Achilles tendon** to the calcaneus; follow the shin to the **tibialis anterior** while your partner dorsiflexes; find the **fibularis tendons** behind the lateral malleolus as they evert; locate the **patella**, **patellar tendon** and **tibial tuberosity**; feel the **MCL** on the inner joint line and the cord-like **LCL** on the outer side with the leg crossed in a figure-four. Say each name aloud as you touch it.',
kase('Skill I practice',{a:{text:'The judge says “calcaneofibular ligament.” Where do you place the label?',choices:[['Just below the tip of the lateral malleolus, running down to the heel','b','Correct. The CFL runs from the fibula to the calcaneus.',true],['In front of the medial malleolus','b','That region is the deltoid ligament.',false]]},b:{text:'Next: “vastus medialis oblique.”',choices:[['On the teardrop-shaped bulge just above and inside the kneecap','c','Correct. The VMO helps track the patella.',true],['On the outside of the thigh','c','That is the vastus lateralis.',false]]},c:{text:'You say “finished” after each label so the judge can move on. Efficient and accurate.',end:true}})]],
["Muscle origins, insertions and actions",["Skill I may ask for a muscle’s **origin** (the more fixed, usually proximal attachment), **insertion** (the more movable, usually distal attachment), **belly** (the thick middle) or **tendon**. Lower-body examples: the **rectus femoris** originates on the **anterior inferior iliac spine** and inserts on the **tibial tuberosity** through the patellar tendon; the **sartorius** runs from the **anterior superior iliac spine** to the medial tibia; the **hamstrings** originate on the **ischial tuberosity**—the biceps femoris inserts on the **fibular head**, and the semitendinosus and semimembranosus on the medial tibia.","The **gastrocnemius** originates on the medial and lateral **femoral condyles** and the **soleus** on the posterior tibia and fibula; both insert on the **calcaneus** via the Achilles tendon. The **tibialis anterior** runs from the lateral tibia to the **medial cuneiform and first metatarsal**; the **fibularis longus** passes under the foot to the first metatarsal and medial cuneiform, while the **fibularis brevis** inserts on the **base of the fifth metatarsal**. Knowing these lines helps you place a label on the right spot quickly.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Origin or insertion","pairs":[["Rectus femoris origin","Anterior inferior iliac spine"],["Sartorius origin","Anterior superior iliac spine"],["Hamstring origin","Ischial tuberosity"],["Biceps femoris insertion","Fibular head"],["Achilles insertion","Calcaneus"],["Fibularis brevis insertion","Base of the fifth metatarsal"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Foot: 7 tarsals, 5 metatarsals, 14 phalanges; four arches; protect the base of the fifth metatarsal; extensor hallucis longus lifts the big toe.','Ankle: tibia (medial malleolus) and fibula (lateral malleolus); ATFL (most sprained), CFL, PTFL, deltoid and tibiofibular ligaments; gastrocnemius and soleus form the Achilles; fibularis muscles evert; tibialis anterior dorsiflexes and inverts.','Knee: femur, tibia, patella; MCL (valgus), LCL (varus), ACL, PCL, patellar tendon; quadriceps (rectus femoris, vastus lateralis, VMO), hamstrings (biceps femoris lateral; semitendinosus and semimembranosus medial), sartorius, gracilis, popliteus.'],terms:[['ATFL','Anterior talofibular ligament, the most commonly sprained ankle ligament.'],['Valgus','A force or position that pushes the knee inward.'],['Pes anserinus','The shared insertion of the sartorius, gracilis and semitendinosus on the medial tibia.']],mcqs:[q('What is a common mechanism of injury for an MCL sprain?','Abduction (valgus) and lower leg rotation on a planted foot','Quick deceleration, cutting, twisting and landing','Falling on the anterior knee while flexed','Adduction and internal rotation of the knee','HOSA sample question.'),q('Which ankle ligament is most commonly sprained?','Anterior talofibular','Deltoid','Posterior talofibular','Anterior tibiofibular','Inversion sprains.'),q('Which muscles form the Achilles tendon?','Gastrocnemius and soleus','Tibialis anterior and posterior','Hamstrings','Fibularis longus and brevis','Insert on the calcaneus.'),q('Which muscles evert the foot?','Fibularis (peroneus) longus and brevis','Tibialis anterior','Soleus','Extensor hallucis longus','Lateral compartment.'),q('Which hamstring is on the lateral side?','Biceps femoris','Semitendinosus','Semimembranosus','Gracilis','Attaches near the fibular head.'),q('How many bones are in the foot?','26','12','33','5','7 tarsals, 5 metatarsals, 14 phalanges.'),q('The ACL is most often torn by:','Sudden deceleration, cutting or landing','A direct blow to the shin','Falling on the elbow','Ankle inversion','Noncontact mechanism.'),q('Which test checks for an Achilles rupture?','Thompson test','Lachman test','Valgus stress test','Neer test','Squeeze the calf.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe the lateral ankle ligaments and the muscles that control the ankle, and explain how this anatomy relates to inversion ankle sprains and Achilles injuries.',rubric:[['Ligaments',['atfl','anterior talofibular','calcaneofibular','posterior talofibular','lateral malleolus','deltoid']],['Muscles',['gastrocnemius','soleus','achilles','tibialis anterior','fibularis','peroneus','evert']],['Inversion sprain',['inversion','plantar flexion','atfl first','cfl','grade']],['Achilles',['calcaneus','plantar flex','push-off','thompson','rupture']]],model:'The lateral ankle has three main ligaments that run from the lateral malleolus of the fibula: the anterior talofibular ligament in front of the malleolus, connecting to the talus; the calcaneofibular ligament below it, running down to the calcaneus; and the posterior talofibular ligament behind it. On the medial side, the broad deltoid ligament resists eversion. The gastrocnemius and soleus join to form the Achilles tendon, which inserts on the calcaneus and plantar flexes the ankle; the tibialis anterior dorsiflexes and inverts; and the fibularis longus and brevis run behind the lateral malleolus and evert the foot, helping resist inversion. An inversion sprain usually happens when the foot rolls inward while plantar flexed, the position in which the ATFL is tight and the ankle is least stable, so the ATFL tears first, followed by the calcaneofibular ligament in more severe sprains; sprains are graded I to III by severity. Weak fibularis muscles and poor balance increase the risk. The Achilles tendon can become inflamed with overuse or rupture during a sudden forceful push-off. A rupture is detected with the Thompson test: squeezing the calf normally plantar flexes the foot, but it does not when the tendon is torn.'},sources:[RULES,OSA_SKEL,OSA_MUSC_LL]};

export const SM3={title:'Upper-body, spine and head anatomy (Skill I)',hook:'A baseball pitcher feels a pop on the inside of the elbow. A football player falls on an outstretched hand and has tenderness in the anatomical snuffbox. A soccer player collides heads with an opponent. The upper body, spine and head have their own landmarks, and Skill I can ask you to find any of them. This lesson completes the Skill I list.',objectives:['Identify the bones, joints, ligaments and muscles of the shoulder.','Identify the structures of the elbow, forearm, wrist and hand.','Identify cervical, thoracic and lumbar vertebrae and the bones of the skull.','Use a fast, systematic method for Skill I.'],sections:[
['The shoulder',[
'The shoulder complex includes the **clavicle**, **scapula** and **humerus** and four joints: the **sternoclavicular (SC)** joint (where the clavicle meets the sternum), the **acromioclavicular (AC)** joint (the bump at the outer end of the clavicle—sprained in a “shoulder separation”), the **glenohumeral (GH)** joint (the ball-and-socket joint, most often dislocated **anteriorly**) and the scapulothoracic articulation. Ligaments on the list: the **coracoclavicular** (from the coracoid process to the clavicle), **coracoacromial** (forms an arch over the rotator cuff) and **acromioclavicular** ligaments.',
'Muscles: the **deltoid** caps the shoulder and abducts the arm; the **rotator cuff** (**supraspinatus**, **infraspinatus**, **teres minor**, **subscapularis**—“SITS”) stabilizes the joint, with the **infraspinatus** and **teres minor** externally rotating. The **teres major** and **latissimus dorsi** extend, adduct and internally rotate. The **trapezius** elevates and retracts the scapula; the **rhomboids major and minor** retract it; the **levator scapulae** elevates it; the **serratus anterior** protracts it and holds it against the ribs (weakness causes “winging”). The **biceps** and **triceps** cross the shoulder and elbow.',
match('Shoulder structures',[['AC joint','Bump at the outer clavicle; separations'],['Glenohumeral joint','Ball-and-socket; usually dislocates anteriorly'],['Coracoclavicular ligament','Coracoid process to clavicle'],['Infraspinatus','Rotator cuff muscle; external rotation'],['Serratus anterior','Holds the scapula to the ribs'],['Latissimus dorsi','Extends, adducts and internally rotates the arm'],['Rhomboids','Retract the scapula'],['Levator scapulae','Elevates the scapula']])]],
['The elbow and forearm',[
'The **humerus**, **radius** (thumb side) and **ulna** (pinky side, forming the point of the elbow—the olecranon) meet at the elbow. The **ulnar collateral ligament (UCL)** on the medial side resists valgus stress and is injured in **throwing athletes** (reconstruction is called “Tommy John” surgery); the **radial collateral ligament** is on the lateral side; the **annular ligament** wraps the radial head so it can rotate.',
'Muscles: the **biceps** flexes the elbow and supinates the forearm; the **triceps** extends it; the **brachioradialis** (the bulge on the thumb side of the forearm) flexes the elbow with the thumb up. The **supinator** turns the palm up; the **pronator teres** (near the elbow) and **pronator quadratus** (near the wrist) turn it down. The **flexor carpi radialis and ulnaris** start on the medial epicondyle and flex the wrist; the **extensor carpi radialis and ulnaris** start near the lateral epicondyle and extend it—overuse of the extensors causes **lateral epicondylitis** (“tennis elbow”) and of the flexors **medial epicondylitis** (“golfer’s elbow”).',
sort('Medial or lateral elbow?',['Medial (pinky side)','Lateral (thumb side)'],[['Ulnar collateral ligament',0],['Radial collateral ligament',1],['Flexor carpi ulnaris origin',0],['Extensor carpi radialis origin',1],['Brachioradialis',1],['Tennis elbow pain',1]])]],
['The wrist and hand',[
'The hand has **8 carpals**, **5 metacarpals** and **14 phalanges** (numbered 1–5 from the thumb). The **anatomical snuffbox** is the hollow on the thumb side of the wrist between tendons when the thumb is extended; **tenderness there suggests a scaphoid fracture**, which can heal poorly because of limited blood supply. The **thenar eminence** is the muscle pad at the base of the thumb and the **hypothenar eminence** the pad at the base of the little finger.',
'Muscles on the list include the **abductor pollicis longus** (moves the thumb away from the palm), the **flexor and extensor pollicis** (bend and straighten the thumb) and the **flexor and extensor digiti minimi** (bend and straighten the little finger). Common injuries: **boxer’s fracture** (fifth metacarpal neck), **mallet finger** (extensor tendon at the fingertip), **jersey finger** (flexor tendon avulsion, often the ring finger) and **gamekeeper’s or skier’s thumb** (UCL of the thumb).',
match('Wrist and hand',[['Anatomical snuffbox','Tenderness suggests scaphoid fracture'],['Thenar eminence','Muscle pad at the base of the thumb'],['Hypothenar eminence','Muscle pad at the base of the little finger'],['Metacarpals','Bones of the palm (5)'],['Abductor pollicis longus','Moves the thumb away from the palm'],['Boxer’s fracture','Fifth metacarpal neck']])]],
['Spine and skull',[
'The spine has **7 cervical** vertebrae (C1 is the atlas, C2 the axis; the prominent bump at the base of the neck is usually **C7**), **12 thoracic** vertebrae (each attached to a pair of ribs) and **5 lumbar** vertebrae, followed by the sacrum and coccyx. A memory aid: breakfast at 7, lunch at 12, dinner at 5. The **spinous processes** can be palpated down the midline; the top of the iliac crests is roughly level with **L4**.',
'Skull bones on the list: **frontal** (forehead), **parietal** (top and sides), **occipital** (back and base), **temporal** (sides, near the ears), **zygomatic** (cheekbones), **nasal** (bridge of the nose), **maxilla** (upper jaw) and **mandible** (lower jaw, the only movable skull bone). Knowing these helps describe facial injuries and concussion mechanisms.',
order('Spine from top to bottom',['Cervical (7)','Thoracic (12)','Lumbar (5)','Sacrum','Coccyx']),
label('skull','Bones of the skull',[['temporal','Temporal bone','Side of the head near the ear.'],['zygomatic','Zygomatic bone','Cheekbone.'],['maxilla','Maxilla','Upper jaw.'],['body','Mandible (body)','Lower jaw.'],['tmj','Temporomandibular joint','Where the mandible meets the temporal bone.']])]],
['Skill I strategy',[
'Skill I gives **2 points per correct landmark**, 21 landmarks in 7 minutes, with **15 seconds** each—the timekeeper stops you at 15 seconds. The judge, playing the head athletic trainer, names one landmark at a time from seven regions: foot; ankle and lower leg; knee; neck, spine and head; shoulder; elbow and forearm; and wrist, hand and fingers. For muscles, you may be asked for the **origin**, **insertion**, **belly** or **tendon**, so know where each muscle starts and ends.',
'Strategy: practice on many body types, start from a **bony landmark you can always find** (malleoli, patella, acromion, olecranon, C7) and move from there; ask the patient to **contract** a muscle to make it visible; place the label precisely and say “**finished**.” If you do not know one, make your best attempt quickly—an empty answer earns nothing.',
flip('Origins and insertions to know',[['Biceps brachii','Scapula (coracoid and supraglenoid) → radial tuberosity'],['Rectus femoris','Anterior inferior iliac spine → tibial tuberosity via the patellar tendon'],['Gastrocnemius','Femoral condyles → calcaneus via the Achilles'],['Sartorius','Anterior superior iliac spine → medial tibia (pes anserinus)'],['Deltoid','Clavicle, acromion and scapular spine → deltoid tuberosity of the humerus'],['Tibialis anterior','Lateral tibia → medial cuneiform and first metatarsal']])]],
["Upper-body muscle attachments",["Upper-body examples: the **biceps brachii** long head starts at the **supraglenoid tubercle** and the short head at the **coracoid process**, inserting on the **radial tuberosity**; the **triceps** inserts on the **olecranon**. The **deltoid** runs from the clavicle, acromion and scapular spine to the **deltoid tuberosity** of the humerus. The **supraspinatus** sits above the scapular spine and inserts on the greater tubercle; the **infraspinatus** and **teres minor** sit below it; the **subscapularis** lies on the front of the scapula and inserts on the lesser tubercle.","The **latissimus dorsi** runs from the lower back and pelvis to the front of the humerus; the **trapezius** from the skull and spine to the clavicle and scapula; the **rhomboids** from the spine to the medial border of the scapula; the **levator scapulae** from the upper cervical vertebrae to the superior angle of the scapula. Forearm flexors start at the **medial epicondyle** and extensors at the **lateral epicondyle**—a quick way to place labels for wrist flexors and extensors.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Medial or lateral epicondyle origin?","buckets":["Medial epicondyle","Lateral epicondyle"],"items":[["Flexor carpi radialis",0],["Extensor carpi radialis brevis",1],["Flexor carpi ulnaris",0],["Extensor carpi ulnaris",1],["Pronator teres",0],["Extensor digitorum",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Shoulder: SC, AC and glenohumeral joints; coracoclavicular, coracoacromial and AC ligaments; deltoid, SITS rotator cuff, teres major, latissimus, trapezius, rhomboids, levator scapulae, serratus anterior.','Elbow and forearm: UCL (throwers), radial collateral and annular ligaments; biceps, triceps, brachioradialis, supinator, pronators and wrist flexors/extensors; wrist and hand: 8 carpals, snuffbox (scaphoid), thenar and hypothenar eminences.','Spine: 7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar; skull: frontal, parietal, occipital, temporal, zygomatic, nasal, maxilla, mandible; Skill I: 21 landmarks, 15 seconds, 2 points each.'],terms:[['Anatomical snuffbox','A hollow on the thumb side of the wrist over the scaphoid.'],['Rotator cuff','Supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor and subscapularis.'],['Ulnar collateral ligament','The medial elbow ligament stressed by throwing.']],mcqs:[q('Tenderness in the anatomical snuffbox suggests a fracture of the:','Scaphoid','Fifth metacarpal','Radius head','Clavicle','Poor blood supply.'),q('A “shoulder separation” injures the:','Acromioclavicular joint','Glenohumeral joint','Sternoclavicular joint','Elbow','AC sprain.'),q('Which ligament is injured in throwing athletes needing Tommy John surgery?','Ulnar collateral ligament','Radial collateral ligament','Annular ligament','ACL','Medial elbow.'),q('How many thoracic vertebrae are there?','12','7','5','4','Each with a rib pair.'),q('Which muscle protracts the scapula and prevents winging?','Serratus anterior','Rhomboid major','Trapezius only','Teres major','Long thoracic nerve.'),q('Which skull bone is the only movable one?','Mandible','Maxilla','Temporal','Zygomatic','Lower jaw.'),q('How long do you have to place each label in Skill I?','15 seconds','5 seconds','1 minute','2 minutes','21 landmarks in 7 minutes.'),q('Which muscle forms the bulge on the thumb side of the forearm?','Brachioradialis','Pronator quadratus','Flexor carpi ulnaris','Supinator','Elbow flexor.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe the joints, ligaments and muscles of the shoulder complex and explain how you would locate three shoulder landmarks quickly on a live patient during Skill I.',rubric:[['Bones and joints',['clavicle','scapula','humerus','sternoclavicular','acromioclavicular','glenohumeral']],['Ligaments',['coracoclavicular','coracoacromial','acromioclavicular']],['Muscles',['deltoid','supraspinatus','infraspinatus','teres','subscapularis','trapezius','rhomboids','serratus','latissimus','levator']],['Locating strategy',['palpate','bony landmark','acromion','contract','15 seconds','finished']]],model:'The shoulder complex is made up of the clavicle, scapula and humerus, which form the sternoclavicular joint where the clavicle meets the sternum, the acromioclavicular joint at the outer end of the clavicle and the glenohumeral ball-and-socket joint. Key ligaments are the acromioclavicular ligament, the coracoclavicular ligament from the coracoid process to the underside of the clavicle and the coracoacromial ligament, which forms an arch over the rotator cuff. The deltoid caps the shoulder and abducts the arm; the rotator cuff—supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor and subscapularis—stabilizes the humeral head, with the infraspinatus and teres minor externally rotating; the teres major and latissimus dorsi extend, adduct and internally rotate; the trapezius elevates and retracts the scapula, the rhomboids retract it, the levator scapulae elevates it and the serratus anterior protracts it and holds it to the ribs. In Skill I, I would start from bony landmarks I can always feel. For the AC joint, I slide my finger along the clavicle until I reach the small step at its outer end. For the infraspinatus, I find the spine of the scapula and place the label on the muscle below it, asking the patient to externally rotate so it contracts. For the deltoid, I find the acromion and place the label on the rounded muscle belly below it. I place each label within 15 seconds and say “finished.”'},sources:[RULES,OSA_UPPER,OSA_MUSC_UL,OSA_SKULL]};

export const SM4={title:'Joint actions and maximum range of motion (Skill II)',hook:'In Skill II you watch a patient (or a photo) hold 15 different poses. For each one you must write the joint, the action and the maximum normal range of motion—and spelling counts. “Dorsiflexion” spelled “dorsi-flection” loses points. This lesson teaches the movement vocabulary and every value on the HOSA range-of-motion list.',objectives:['Name joint actions correctly, including special movements of the foot, forearm and wrist.','Recall the HOSA maximum range-of-motion values for each joint action.','Spell joint names and actions accurately.','Relate range-of-motion testing to injury evaluation.'],sections:[
['Movement vocabulary',[
'Movements are described from **anatomical position**. **Flexion** decreases a joint angle; **extension** increases it. **Abduction** moves away from the midline; **adduction** toward it. **Internal (medial) rotation** turns toward the midline and **external (lateral) rotation** away. At the forearm, **pronation** turns the palm down and **supination** turns it up. At the ankle, **dorsiflexion** brings the foot up toward the shin and **plantarflexion** points it down. **Inversion** turns the sole inward and **eversion** outward. At the wrist, **radial deviation (abduction)** moves toward the thumb and **ulnar deviation (adduction)** toward the little finger.',
'For Skill II you earn **2 points** for the joint name and spelling, **2 points** for the action and spelling and **1 point** for the correct range of motion (75 possible). Skill II has **15 poses in 15 minutes**; with a live patient you tell the patient when you are ready for the next pose and may ask for poses to be repeated.',
match('Action and description',[['Dorsiflexion','Foot up toward the shin'],['Plantarflexion','Foot pointed down'],['Inversion','Sole turned inward'],['Eversion','Sole turned outward'],['Pronation','Palm turned down'],['Supination','Palm turned up'],['Radial deviation','Hand toward the thumb side'],['Ulnar deviation','Hand toward the little-finger side']])]],
['Lower-extremity ROM values',[
'HOSA’s Skill II list for the lower extremity: **foot/ankle eversion 20°**, **foot/ankle inversion 40°**, **ankle dorsiflexion 20°**, **ankle plantarflexion 45°**, **knee flexion 140°**, **hip flexion 125°**, **hip extension 10°**, **hip abduction 45°**, **hip adduction 40°**, **hip internal rotation 45°** and **hip external rotation 45°**. Note that these are the HOSA reference values; other textbooks may list slightly different numbers, but use these in the event.',
'Memory tips: the ankle goes **down more than up** (plantarflexion 45 vs. dorsiflexion 20) and **in more than out** (inversion 40 vs. eversion 20). The hip **flexes far** (125) but barely **extends** (10). Hip abduction, internal rotation and external rotation are all **45**, and adduction is 40.',
{t:'table',caption:'HOSA maximum ROM — lower extremity',head:['Joint and action','Maximum ROM'],rows:[['Ankle dorsiflexion','20°'],['Ankle plantarflexion','45°'],['Foot/ankle inversion','40°'],['Foot/ankle eversion','20°'],['Knee flexion','140°'],['Hip flexion','125°'],['Hip extension','10°'],['Hip abduction','45°'],['Hip adduction','40°'],['Hip internal rotation','45°'],['Hip external rotation','45°']]},
match('Lower-extremity ROM',[['Ankle plantarflexion','45°'],['Ankle dorsiflexion','20°'],['Foot inversion','40°'],['Foot eversion','20°'],['Knee flexion','140°'],['Hip flexion','125°'],['Hip extension','10°'],['Hip adduction','40°']])]],
['Upper-extremity ROM values',[
'HOSA’s list for the upper extremity: **shoulder flexion 180°**, **shoulder extension 50°**, **shoulder abduction 180°**, **shoulder adduction 40°**, **shoulder internal rotation 90°**, **shoulder external rotation 90°**, **elbow flexion 145°**, **forearm pronation 80°**, **forearm supination 85°**, **wrist flexion 80°**, **wrist extension 70°**, **wrist radial deviation (abduction) 20°** and **wrist ulnar deviation (adduction) 45°**.',
'Memory tips: the shoulder **flexes and abducts to 180** (straight overhead) and **rotates 90** each way. **Supination (85)** is slightly more than **pronation (80)**. The wrist **flexes 80** and **extends 70**, and deviates **more toward the ulna (45)** than toward the radius (20).',
{t:'table',caption:'HOSA maximum ROM — upper extremity',head:['Joint and action','Maximum ROM'],rows:[['Shoulder flexion','180°'],['Shoulder extension','50°'],['Shoulder abduction','180°'],['Shoulder adduction','40°'],['Shoulder internal rotation','90°'],['Shoulder external rotation','90°'],['Elbow flexion','145°'],['Forearm pronation','80°'],['Forearm supination','85°'],['Wrist flexion','80°'],['Wrist extension','70°'],['Wrist radial deviation','20°'],['Wrist ulnar deviation','45°']]},
match('Upper-extremity ROM',[['Shoulder flexion','180°'],['Shoulder extension','50°'],['Shoulder internal rotation','90°'],['Elbow flexion','145°'],['Forearm pronation','80°'],['Forearm supination','85°'],['Wrist extension','70°'],['Wrist ulnar deviation','45°']])]],
['Spelling and joint names',[
'Spelling counts for both the **joint** and the **action**. Practice writing: **glenohumeral** or **shoulder**, **elbow**, **radioulnar** (forearm), **wrist** (radiocarpal), **hip** (acetabulofemoral), **knee** (tibiofemoral), **ankle** (talocrural) and **subtalar** (where inversion and eversion mainly occur). Common misspellings: “dorsiflexion” (one word), “plantarflexion” (one word, or “plantar flexion”), “supination,” “pronation,” “abduction” (with a b), “adduction” (with two d’s), “eversion,” “inversion” and “deviation.”',
'Use the joint name the judge expects for the pose—usually the common name (shoulder, elbow, wrist, hip, knee, ankle) or the event list wording (“foot/ankle” for inversion and eversion, “forearm” for pronation and supination). Write neatly so judges can read every letter.',
sort('Spelled correctly?',['Correct','Misspelled'],[['Dorsiflexion',0],['Plantarflection',1],['Supination',0],['Abbduction',1],['Adduction',0],['Inversion',0],['Pronnation',1],['Eversion',0]])]],
['Using ROM in evaluation',[
'ROM is measured with a **goniometer**: the **axis** sits over the joint, the **stationary arm** along the proximal segment and the **moving arm** along the distal segment. **Active ROM** (the athlete moves) tests willingness and muscle function; **passive ROM** (the examiner moves) tests the joint and its tissues; **resistive** testing checks strength. Pain with active and resisted movement suggests a muscle or tendon problem; pain with passive stretch suggests ligament, capsule or stretched muscle involvement. Always compare with the **uninjured side**.',
'**End feel** describes what the examiner feels at the end of passive motion: **soft** (tissue approximation, like elbow flexion), **firm** (capsular or ligament stretch) or **hard** (bone on bone, like elbow extension). An **empty** end feel (pain stops motion before an end is felt) or a **springy block** suggests a significant problem. ROM goals guide rehabilitation and return to play.',
kase('Skill II practice',{a:{text:'The patient raises a straight arm forward and fully overhead. What do you write?',choices:[['Shoulder; flexion; 180°','b','Correct.',true],['Shoulder; abduction; 180°','b','Abduction is out to the side.',false]]},b:{text:'Next pose: the patient turns the sole of the foot inward.',choices:[['Foot/ankle; inversion; 40°','c','Correct.',true],['Ankle; eversion; 20°','c','Eversion turns the sole outward.',false]]},c:{text:'You write each answer neatly, check spelling and move on when ready.',end:true}})]],
["Practicing for Skill II",["Practice with a partner or photos. Have your partner hold a pose; first decide the **joint**, then the **plane of movement** (forward and back is usually flexion and extension; out to the side is abduction and adduction; turning is rotation, pronation or supination), then recall the **number**. Write the joint and action in full words and check spelling before moving on. Group values you tend to confuse—for example, hip abduction 45 versus shoulder adduction 40.","Watch for **compensation** in live poses: a patient leaning the trunk may make shoulder abduction look different, and turning the whole leg can mimic ankle inversion. Focus on the joint being moved. If allowed, ask the patient to repeat a pose; poses do not need to be answered in order, so skip a hard one and return to it.",{"t":"check","q":"A patient lifts the leg straight out to the side. What do you write?","options":["Hip; abduction; 45°","Hip; adduction; 40°","Knee; flexion; 140°","Hip; flexion; 125°"],"why":"Out to the side is abduction; HOSA lists 45°."}]]
],takeaways:['Know flexion/extension, abduction/adduction, internal/external rotation, pronation/supination, dorsiflexion/plantarflexion, inversion/eversion and radial/ulnar deviation; Skill II gives 2 + 2 + 1 points for joint, action and ROM, and spelling counts.','HOSA lower-extremity values: dorsiflexion 20, plantarflexion 45, inversion 40, eversion 20, knee flexion 140, hip flexion 125, extension 10, abduction 45, adduction 40, internal and external rotation 45.','Upper-extremity values: shoulder flexion and abduction 180, extension 50, adduction 40, rotations 90; elbow flexion 145; pronation 80, supination 85; wrist flexion 80, extension 70, radial 20, ulnar 45.'],terms:[['Goniometer','A tool that measures joint angles.'],['End feel','The sensation felt at the end of passive range of motion.'],['Plantarflexion','Pointing the foot downward.']],mcqs:[q('According to the HOSA list, what is maximum ankle plantarflexion?','45°','20°','90°','10°','Dorsiflexion is 20°.'),q('Maximum knee flexion on the HOSA list is:','140°','90°','180°','120°','Per Skill II.'),q('Maximum hip extension is:','10°','45°','90°','125°','Very limited.'),q('Maximum shoulder external rotation is:','90°','45°','180°','40°','Internal rotation is also 90°.'),q('Maximum forearm supination is:','85°','80°','90°','45°','Pronation is 80°.'),q('Maximum wrist ulnar deviation is:','45°','20°','80°','70°','Radial deviation is 20°.'),q('Which spelling is correct?','Dorsiflexion','Dorsi-flection','Dorsyflexion','Dorseflexion','One word.'),q('Turning the palm down is:','Pronation','Supination','Inversion','Abduction','Palm down.')],frq:{prompt:'For each of these poses, write the joint, action and HOSA maximum ROM, then explain how active and passive ROM testing help evaluate an injury: (1) arm lifted out to the side overhead, (2) foot pointed down, (3) hand bent back, (4) knee bent fully, (5) palm turned up.',rubric:[['Poses 1–2',['shoulder','abduction','180','ankle','plantarflexion','45']],['Poses 3–5',['wrist','extension','70','knee','flexion','140','forearm','supination','85']],['Active vs passive',['active','passive','muscle','tendon','ligament','capsule','compare']],['Evaluation details',['goniometer','end feel','uninjured side','pain','resisted']]],model:'Pose 1 is the shoulder in abduction, with a maximum of 180 degrees. Pose 2 is the ankle in plantarflexion, 45 degrees. Pose 3 is the wrist in extension, 70 degrees. Pose 4 is the knee in flexion, 140 degrees. Pose 5 is the forearm in supination, 85 degrees. During an injury evaluation, I measure range of motion with a goniometer, placing the axis over the joint, the stationary arm along the proximal segment and the moving arm along the distal segment, and I always compare with the uninjured side. Active range of motion, in which the athlete moves the joint, shows their willingness to move and how well the muscles and tendons work; pain or weakness with active and resisted motion suggests a muscle or tendon injury such as a strain. Passive range of motion, in which I move the joint while the athlete relaxes, tests the joint surfaces, ligaments and capsule; pain or limited motion with passive stretch suggests a ligament sprain, capsular tightness or a stretched muscle. The end feel at the limit of passive motion—soft, firm, hard or empty—gives further clues, and measured range-of-motion values set goals for rehabilitation and return to play.'},sources:[RULES,OSA_MOVE]};

export const SM5={title:'Injury and illness prevention and wellness promotion',hook:'The best injury is the one that never happens. Proper conditioning, fitted equipment, good nutrition and hydration, and smart decisions about heat and lightning prevent far more harm than any treatment. Injury and illness prevention and wellness promotion is 30% of the test—the largest single domain.',objectives:['Design conditioning programs using warm-up, flexibility, strength, endurance and periodization principles.','Apply nutrition and hydration principles for athletes, including vitamins and minerals.','Fit protective equipment and manage environmental risks such as heat, cold and lightning.','Recognize medical conditions and wellness issues that affect participation.'],sections:[
['Conditioning and flexibility',[
'A good program begins with a **dynamic warm-up** (light jogging, leg swings, skips) that raises muscle temperature and prepares movement patterns; long **static stretching** is better saved for after activity or separate flexibility sessions. Training follows the **FITT** principle—**F**requency, **I**ntensity, **T**ime and **T**ype—and **specificity** (train the way you play), **overload** (gradually increase demands) and **reversibility** (fitness fades without training). **Periodization** divides the year into off-season, preseason, in-season and transition phases with planned changes in volume and intensity to peak at the right time and avoid overtraining.',
'Strength training improves joint stability and reduces injury risk; **neuromuscular training programs** that teach landing and cutting mechanics (soft knees, knees over toes, avoiding knee valgus) reduce ACL injuries, especially in female athletes. Endurance training builds the aerobic base. **Rest and sleep** allow recovery; overtraining causes fatigue, poor performance and injury. Young athletes benefit from playing multiple sports rather than **early specialization**, which increases overuse injuries.',
match('Training principle',[['Specificity','Train the way you play'],['Overload','Gradually increase demands'],['Reversibility','Fitness fades without training'],['Periodization','Planned training phases across the year'],['Dynamic warm-up','Movement-based preparation before activity'],['Neuromuscular training','Teaches safe landing and cutting to reduce ACL injuries']])]],
['Nutrition and hydration',[
'Athletes need **carbohydrates** (main fuel for high-intensity exercise, stored as glycogen), **protein** (muscle repair and growth—about 1.2–2.0 g/kg/day for many athletes), **fats** (energy for long, lower-intensity exercise and hormone production), **vitamins**, **minerals** and **water**. **Thiamine (vitamin B₁)** is essential for metabolizing carbohydrates and some amino acids, maintaining a normal appetite and supporting nervous-system function. **Riboflavin (B₂)** and **niacin (B₃)** help release energy; **vitamin C** supports collagen and wound healing; **vitamin D** and **calcium** build bone; **iron** carries oxygen (iron deficiency is common in female endurance athletes).',
'**Hydration**: start practice well hydrated, drink during activity and weigh before and after—replace about **16–24 ounces of fluid per pound lost**. Urine color (pale yellow) is a simple check. Sports drinks with carbohydrates and sodium help during long or hot sessions. **Low energy availability** (not eating enough for training demands) can cause the **female athlete triad**—low energy availability, menstrual dysfunction and low bone mineral density—now part of **relative energy deficiency in sport (RED-S)**, which affects athletes of any gender.',
match('Nutrient and role',[['Thiamine (B₁)','Carbohydrate metabolism, appetite and nerve function'],['Vitamin C','Collagen formation and healing'],['Iron','Carries oxygen in hemoglobin'],['Calcium and vitamin D','Bone strength'],['Carbohydrate','Main fuel for intense exercise'],['Protein','Muscle repair and growth']]),
chk('An athlete loses 3 pounds during practice. How much fluid should they drink afterward?',['About 48–72 ounces','About 8 ounces','None if not thirsty','About 1 gallon per pound'],'16–24 oz per pound × 3 pounds.')]],
['Protective equipment',[
'Equipment must meet safety standards—football and lacrosse **helmets** are certified by **NOCSAE**—and must **fit properly**. A football helmet should fit snugly with the cheek pads touching the face, the front about **one inch above the eyebrows**, the chin strap centered and snug, and the ear holes aligned with the ears; check air bladders and reconditioning dates. **Shoulder pads** should cover the AC joint and the deltoid, allow full arm motion and sit snugly. **Mouth guards** reduce dental injuries; **eyewear** protects in racquet sports; **padding** and **braces** protect injured areas.',
'Footwear should match the sport and surface, fit at the end of the day when feet are largest, and be replaced when worn. Prophylactic **ankle braces** and taping reduce repeat ankle sprains. ATs teach athletes and coaches to inspect equipment regularly and to report damage.',
flip('Equipment fit checks',[['Helmet front','About 1 inch above the eyebrows'],['Helmet cheek pads','Snug against the face'],['Chin strap','Centered and snug'],['Shoulder pads','Cover the AC joint and deltoid'],['Mouth guard','Protects teeth and jaw'],['Shoes','Fit at the end of the day; replace when worn']])]],
['Environmental conditions',[
'**Heat illness** risk rises with temperature, humidity, sun, poor fitness and lack of **acclimatization**. The **wet-bulb globe temperature (WBGT)** combines heat, humidity, sun and wind to guide activity modifications. **Heat acclimatization** over about **10–14 days** gradually increases practice length, intensity and equipment. Prevention includes scheduled water breaks, shade, removing equipment in high heat, rest periods and identifying at-risk athletes. **Exertional heat stroke**—core temperature above about **40 °C (104 °F)** with central nervous system changes—requires immediate **cold-water immersion** (“cool first, transport second”).',
'**Lightning**: when thunder is heard or lightning is seen, **everyone goes to a safe structure or fully enclosed vehicle**; activity resumes only **30 minutes after the last thunder or lightning**. Dugouts, open shelters and trees are not safe. **Cold** conditions risk **hypothermia** and **frostbite**—dress in layers, stay dry and limit exposure. Air quality alerts may also require modifying outdoor activity.',
sort('Safe practice or unsafe?',['Safe','Unsafe'],[['Waiting 30 minutes after the last thunder to resume',0],['Sheltering under a tall tree during lightning',1],['Gradual 10–14 day heat acclimatization',0],['Full pads on the first day of hot preseason',1],['Weighing athletes before and after practice',0],['Withholding water as punishment',1]])]],
['Medical conditions and wellness',[
'Athletes with **asthma** should have a rescue inhaler available and an action plan; those with **diabetes** need glucose monitoring and fast-acting sugar on hand; those with **epilepsy**, **allergies** (epinephrine auto-injectors) and **heart conditions** need individualized plans. **Sickle cell trait** can cause **exertional sickling** during intense exercise—screening, gradual conditioning, hydration and rest breaks reduce risk. **Skin infections** such as **MRSA**, **herpes gladiatorum** and **ringworm** spread in contact sports; showering, not sharing towels and cleaning mats prevent them.',
'Wellness includes **mental health**: anxiety, depression, burnout and disordered eating are common in athletes, and ATs help connect athletes to counselors and sports psychologists. Education about **concussion**, **performance-enhancing drugs**, **sleep**, **sun protection** and **substance use** promotes lifelong health.',
"**Pre-season baseline testing** (symptom scores, balance and cognitive tests) helps with later concussion evaluation, and **injury surveillance**—tracking when and how injuries occur—shows which prevention steps are working. Coaches who teach proper technique, such as heads-up tackling and safe landing, prevent injuries at their source.",
match('Condition and prevention',[['Sickle cell trait','Gradual conditioning, hydration and rest breaks'],['Asthma','Rescue inhaler and action plan'],['Food allergy','Epinephrine auto-injector available'],['MRSA in wrestling','Shower, no shared towels, clean mats'],['Type 1 diabetes','Glucose monitoring and fast sugar on hand'],['Burnout','Rest, variety and mental health support']])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Preseason planning',{a:{text:'It is the first week of August football practice. The WBGT is high and many players are new. What is the best plan? (Scenario invented for practice.)',choices:[['Follow a heat-acclimatization schedule: shorter practices, helmets only at first, frequent water and shade breaks','b','Correct. Gradual acclimatization prevents heat illness.',true],['Full pads, two-a-days and limited water to build toughness','b','That greatly increases heat-illness risk.',false]]},b:{text:'During practice, thunder rumbles in the distance. What do you do?',choices:[['Move everyone to a safe building now and wait 30 minutes after the last thunder','c','Correct. When thunder roars, go indoors.',true],['Finish the drill first','c','Seek shelter immediately.',false]]},c:{text:'Practice resumes 30 minutes after the last thunder. You record weights and fluid intake for every player.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Dynamic warm-up, FITT, specificity, overload, periodization, neuromuscular training and rest prevent injuries; avoid early sport specialization.','Carbohydrates fuel intense exercise, protein repairs muscle; thiamine (B₁) supports carbohydrate metabolism, appetite and nerves; replace 16–24 oz per pound lost; watch for RED-S and the female athlete triad.','Fit NOCSAE-certified helmets (1 inch above the eyebrows) and pads properly; acclimatize to heat over 10–14 days and cool first for heat stroke; shelter from lightning until 30 minutes after the last thunder.'],terms:[['Periodization','Planned variation of training across phases of the year.'],['WBGT','Wet-bulb globe temperature, a measure of heat stress.'],['Heat acclimatization','Gradual adaptation to exercising in heat over about 10–14 days.']],mcqs:[q('Which vitamin is essential for carbohydrate metabolism, normal appetite and nervous system function?','Thiamine','Ascorbic acid','Niacin','Riboflavin','HOSA sample question.'),q('How long after the last thunder should play resume?','30 minutes','5 minutes','2 hours','Immediately','Lightning safety.'),q('How much fluid should be replaced per pound lost?','16–24 ounces','2 ounces','64 ounces','None','Weigh before and after.'),q('Which organization certifies football helmets?','NOCSAE','NATA','BOC','OSHA','Safety standard.'),q('Heat acclimatization typically takes about:','10–14 days','1 day','6 months','2 hours','Gradual.'),q('The female athlete triad includes:','Low energy availability, menstrual dysfunction and low bone density','High blood pressure, diabetes and obesity','Asthma, allergies and eczema','Concussion, fracture and sprain','Part of RED-S.'),q('Which training principle means “train the way you play”?','Specificity','Reversibility','Overload','Recovery','Sport-specific.'),q('Where should the front of a football helmet sit?','About 1 inch above the eyebrows','At the eyebrows','3 inches above the eyebrows','Covering the eyes','Fit check.')],frq:{prompt:'Design an injury and illness prevention plan for a high school soccer team’s preseason, including conditioning, nutrition and hydration, equipment and environmental safety.',rubric:[['Conditioning',['dynamic warm-up','neuromuscular','acl','strength','periodization','gradual','rest']],['Nutrition and hydration',['carbohydrate','protein','weigh','16-24','urine','energy availability']],['Equipment',['shin guards','shoes','mouth guard','fit','inspect']],['Environment and medical',['heat','acclimatization','wbgt','lightning','30 minutes','pre-participation','asthma','sickle cell','eap']]],model:'Before the season, every athlete completes a pre-participation exam to identify conditions such as asthma, heart warning signs, previous injuries and sickle cell trait, and the team rehearses the emergency action plan. Conditioning starts gradually and follows periodization, increasing volume and intensity over the preseason, with a dynamic warm-up before every session and a neuromuscular program that teaches safe landing, cutting and deceleration to reduce ACL and ankle injuries, plus strength, endurance and planned rest days. For nutrition, athletes eat enough total energy, with carbohydrates for fuel and protein for repair, and get calcium, vitamin D, iron and B vitamins from a balanced diet; I would educate them about the signs of low energy availability. For hydration, athletes start practice hydrated, drink during scheduled breaks, check urine color and weigh before and after practice, replacing 16–24 ounces of fluid for every pound lost. Equipment includes properly fitted shin guards, sport-appropriate cleats that are not worn out and mouth guards, inspected regularly. For the environment, a 10–14 day heat-acclimatization schedule, WBGT monitoring with modified practices, shade, ice towels and a cold-water immersion tub prepare for heat illness, and the team goes indoors at the first thunder or lightning and waits 30 minutes after the last.'},sources:[RULES,NATA_HEAT,NIH_ODS,CDC_ACTIVITY,NWS_LIGHTNING]};
